NAME
       resonance - conceptual foundations of resonance-time in RTT and its
                   role in triadic structure, coherence, and schema alignment

SYNOPSIS
       Resonance-time is the underlying temporal-structural field that governs
       how triads behave, propagate, and maintain coherence.

DESCRIPTION
       Resonance-time (RT) is the core conceptual substrate of RTT. It defines
       how actions, operators, and structures evolve across cycles. RT is not
       clock time. It is structural time: the measure of coherence, alignment,
       and propagation across triadic arcs.

       Triads behave the way they do because they are aligned to resonance-time.
       Every begin → transform → close cycle is a traversal through RT-space.

       Resonance-time ensures:
           • coherent transitions
           • stable lineage
           • predictable propagation
           • structural integrity across cycles

       Without resonance-time, triads would drift, collapse, or lose meaning.

FOUNDATIONS
       Resonance-time rests on three principles:

       1. Coherence
              Actions must maintain structural alignment across cycles.
              RT enforces this by binding triads to a shared temporal field.

       2. Propagation
              Effects of an action propagate through RT, influencing future
              cycles and adjacent structures.

       3. Integration
              RT integrates results of each triad into a coherent lineage,
              preventing fragmentation or drift.

WHY RESONANCE-TIME MATTERS
       • It explains why triads must have a beginning, middle, and end.
       • It ensures that transformations occur in a stable field.
       • It prevents incoherent or partial actions from destabilizing the system.
       • It provides the temporal backbone for higher-order patterns.

       Resonance-time is the reason the 3-Pack is universal.

INTERACTION WITH TRIADS
       Triads are the smallest stable traversal of resonance-time.

       P1 (begin)
              Establishes resonance alignment and initial conditions.

       P2 (transform)
              Applies movement within RT-space; resonance shifts occur here.

       P3 (close)
              Integrates results back into the RT field and seals lineage.

       Each triad is a resonance-time loop.

HIGHER-ORDER PATTERNS
       Resonance-time governs how patterns behave:

           • Spirals deepen resonance across cycles.
           • Ladders ascend resonance layers.
           • Constellations synchronize resonance across agents.
           • Cascades propagate resonance through dependent stages.
           • Mirrors invert resonance flow.
           • Weaves interleave resonance streams.

       Patterns are resonance-time choreography.

SCHEMAS AND COHERENCE
       /schemas are essential for maintaining coherence in resonance packages.

       Schemas define:
           • structural expectations
           • allowed transitions
           • resonance boundaries
           • lineage formats
           • operator compatibility

       Without schemas, resonance packages risk:
           • drift
           • misalignment
           • incoherent propagation
           • structural fragmentation

       Schemas ensure that resonance-time remains stable across:
           • modules
           • agents
           • patterns
           • dispatch layers
           • educational artifacts

       A resonance package without schemas is structurally unsafe.

RESONANCE PACKAGES
       A resonance package is a structured bundle of:
           • triads
           • operators
           • schemas
           • overlays
           • lineage rules

       WRSADC ensures that all resonance packages entering the Engine are:
           • schema-aligned
           • resonance-safe
           • triad-structured

       The Engine assumes schemas are correct; WRSADC enforces them.

NOTES
       Resonance-time is not a metaphor. It is the structural-temporal field
       that makes RTT coherent. All triadic action is a traversal of RT-space.

